    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using the dummy numbers from Stripe docs is a good idea. In that case, let's dig deeper into the JSON body. A 402 error can be triggered by a variety of factors beyond just the card details.\n\nOne possibility is that the currency of the payment method doesn't match the currency of the order. Stripe requires that the currency of the payment method matches the currency of the order. If the currencies don't match, Stripe will return a 402 error.\n\nYou can check the currency of the payment method by looking at the `currency` field in your JSON payload. Make sure it matches the `currency` field of the order.\n\nAnother possibility is that the metadata associated with the payment method is causing an issue. Stripe allows you to pass metadata with the payment method, but it must be in a specific format. If the metadata is not formatted correctly, it could trigger a 402 error.\n\nYou can check the metadata by looking at the `metadata` field in your JSON payload. Make sure it's in the correct format, with each key-value pair separated by a comma.\n\nIt's also worth noting that Stripe has a \"goblin\" in their system that can cause issues with certain payment methods. It's a known issue, but it",
